#version 450
// Which faces owe work this frame, packed into a list the shading pass is dispatched over.
//
// # Why a pass of its own
//
// `shade_faces.comp` was dispatched over every live slot, and an invocation with nothing to do read
// thirty-two bytes and stopped. That was the right trade while most of the store was working: what
// costs is the march, not the record.
//
// It stopped being the right trade the moment light stopped at what a pixel had read. Then the
// store held 772,000 live faces of which 220,000 owed anything -- about eighteen busy lanes in a
// workgroup of sixty-four -- and a workgroup runs for as long as its slowest lane, so five sixths
// of the machine sat waiting on the other sixth. The idle invocations cost almost no bandwidth and
// most of the occupancy.
//
// Packing them is the whole of this pass. It reads the same thirty-two bytes the shading pass would
// have read, decides with the same function (`face_work_of`, in node.glsl, so the two cannot answer
// differently), and appends the slot. The shading pass is then dispatched indirectly over the
// count, and every workgroup it launches is full.
//
// # The atomic is per workgroup, not per face
//
// Two hundred thousand atomicAdds on one word a frame would serialise on that word. Each workgroup
// counts its own owed slots in shared memory, takes ONE atomicAdd for the whole block, and writes
// its members at that base. The order faces end up in is therefore arbitrary between workgroups
// and ascending within one, which nothing downstream depends on -- a face's answer is a function
// of the face.
//
// # ...and R4b's coverage rule, which is here for a reason and not for convenience
//
// This is the one pass that walks the WHOLE store once a frame and is not the shading pass. That
// makes it the place a per-face decision about the lobe POOL can be taken before the shading pass
// acts on it: `node_face_lobe` runs inside the shading dispatch and its common path is "this face
// already holds a block, hand it back", so a block placed here is simply found there. The barrier
// between the two dispatches is already a full shader-write to shader-read one, because the list is
// both data and a dispatch command, so nothing new is needed to order it.
//
// The alternative was to size the block inside `node_face_lobe` itself, and it is worse for a
// reason that has nothing to do with which file it lives in: that function runs once per VISIT of a
// face, which is one frame in `face_stride`, while the coverage of a face changes on every frame
// the camera moves. Sizing it here re-asks the question at the frame rate for every live face, out
// of a walk this pass was making anyway.
#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive : require

layout(local_size_x = 64) in;

#include "node.glsl"

// ---- R4b: the bin count a face earns, from its coverage and its roughness ----------------------
//
// D186's rule, and the half of R4b that was never built. `face_bins_earned` in
// src/world/face_store.hpp carries the argument in full and the suite tests it there; this is the
// same three lines, where the camera is.
//
// Must match kProbeCoverageBins in src/gpu/render_params.hpp. Spelled here rather than beside the
// other dials in node.glsl because node.glsl belongs to the passes that READ a lobe, and this bit
// is only ever asked about by the pass that sizes one.
const uint kProbeCoverageBins = 1u << 15;   // 15 and not 14: it collided with kProbeRefractStack, D736
bool probe_coverage_bins() { return (light_probe.words[0] & kProbeCoverageBins) != 0u; }

// How many pixels this face covers, measured rather than guessed.
//
// `node_face_coverage` is the pool's VOLUMETRIC fill fraction and is a different quantity entirely.
// What this wants is the screen, and the screen needs three things the record already has: the
// face's own extent, which is its level; the distance to it, which is its coordinate against the
// camera's; and what one pixel subtends, which is the lens. Everything is in voxels, so the extent
// and the distance cancel their units and only the angle carries one.
//
// **The distance is taken to the face's COARSE PATCH and not to the face**, and that is the whole
// of what D599 got wrong. Coverage varies smoothly across one flat surface, so a decision taken per
// face flips face to face along the middle of it -- and R5's cross-face blend pairs a lobe only
// with lobes cut into the same number of directions, so exactly the faces at that boundary get no
// blending and stand out as noise. D387's standing measurement is that a hard per-face decision on
// a smooth quantity is a new discontinuity per face. Shifting the coordinate down by
// `kFaceAncestorStep` makes five hundred and twelve fine faces compute one distance and therefore
// one class, by construction: the boundary between classes becomes a step at a patch edge, which
// the blend can pair either side of, rather than a speckle through the middle of a surface.
float face_coverage_pixels(Face face) {
    const uint level = face_level_of(face.packed);
    const float extent = float(1u << level);
    // The patch this face is in, three levels up: the same shift the host takes for a stand-in.
    // Not called `patch` — that is a reserved word in GLSL and the error it gives names the line
    // after it, which is a wasted build.
    const ivec3 coarse = ivec3(face.x, face.y, face.z) >> kFaceAncestorStep;
    const float coarse_extent = extent * float(1 << kFaceAncestorStep);
    const vec3 centre = (vec3(coarse) + 0.5) * coarse_extent - vec3(push.camera_chunk.xyz * 256);
    // Never nearer than the face is wide, so a camera inside the geometry cannot divide by nothing.
    const float reach = max(length(centre - push.origin.xyz), extent);
    // The screen's own pixel, not the marcher's -- `push.lens.z` is the detail bias, and biasing
    // this would make the rule a function of a dial about something else. The same expression
    // shade_faces.comp takes for its rays.
    const float pixel_angle = max(2.0 * push.lens.x / float(push.resolution.y), 1e-9);
    const float across = extent / (reach * pixel_angle);
    return across * across;
}

// ...and the rule, applied to the pool.
//
// It asks for the SHARP class and never for the cheap one, because the cheap one is the floor: a
// face that earns less than a big block is left exactly as it was and the shading pass gives it the
// one block it has always given it. So the arm this ships under adds allocation where the rule
// earns it and takes none away, which is what makes `--no-coverage-bins` restore today's fixed
// allocation to the byte rather than approximately.
//
// A face that earns nothing at all -- matte stone, which is most of a world -- is turned away by
// `kLobeWorthFloor` before any of this, exactly as it always was.
void face_coverage_bins(uint slot, Face face) {
    if (!probe_coverage_bins() || !probe_lobe()) return;
    if (!face_live_of(face.packed)) return;
    if (slot >= kLobeSlotMask) return;

    // Read only. What this face is made of is worked out by the SHADING pass and kept, and a face
    // this pass reaches before that has ever happened simply has no class this frame and gets one
    // the next -- which is one frame of the cheap block on a face that is about to be given four,
    // against this pass taking a descent of its own to answer a question already being answered.
    if (slot >= face_material.words.length()) return;
    const uint material = face_material.words[slot];
    if ((material & kFaceMaterialKnown) == 0u) return;

    const uint rough = face_material_rough(material);
    const float worth = face_lobe_worth(rough, face_material_metal(material));
    const float floor_worth = push.tone.z >= 0.0 ? push.tone.z : kLobeWorthFloor;
    if (worth < floor_worth) return;

    // The two quantities, and the smaller of them is what the face has earned. No cutoff on either:
    // what quantises the answer is the pool's own block, which is where a bin count has to be
    // quantised.
    const float earned = min(face_lobe_bins_wanted(rough), face_coverage_pixels(face));
    if (earned < float(kLobeBigBins)) return;

    const uint base = face_lobe_set(slot);
    const uint mine = face_lobe_holder(slot, worth);
    const uint frame = node_push.frame;

    // Already holding something? A run is idempotent -- stamp it and stop. A single block is the
    // cheap class and is what this is here to upgrade, so it falls through, and the way it is in is
    // remembered so the take-over below can treat it as free to itself.
    //
    // ...and a run may have been BROKEN under this face since the last frame, which is the one
    // state the sharp class can reach that the cheap class cannot. `node_face_lobe`'s ordinary
    // take-over may take any way of a set that is cold or held by something worth less, and a run's
    // HEAD is an ordinary way to that walk: it clears the head's class word and hands the block to
    // the new face, and the three followers are left naming a slot that no longer holds the head.
    // A reader landing on one of those steps back to the head and reads somebody else's bins as its
    // own -- and would read thirty-six of them as a hundred and forty-four if the class word had
    // survived, which is the fault the class word is cleared to prevent and is only half of it.
    //
    // So the run is checked, not assumed, on every frame by the pass that made it. A broken run is
    // released outright: this face then has nothing, and the shading pass gives it a clean cheap
    // block on the same frame, which is a coarser lobe for one visit rather than a wrong one.
    uint mine_at = kLobeWays;
    bool broken = false;
    for (uint way = 0u; way < kLobeWays; ++way) {
        const uint header = face_lobe_header(base + way);
        if (header + 1u >= face_lobe.words.length()) return;
        const uint held = face_lobe.words[header];
        if (!face_lobe_is_held(held) || face_lobe_holder_slot(held) != slot) continue;
        const uint state = face_lobe.words[face_lobe_state_at(base + way)];
        if ((state & (kLobeBig | kLobeFollower)) == 0u) {
            mine_at = way;   // the cheap class, which is what this is here to upgrade
            break;
        }
        const uint head = base + (way & ~(kLobeBigWays - 1u));
        const uint head_held = face_lobe.words[face_lobe_header(head)];
        const uint head_state = face_lobe.words[face_lobe_state_at(head)];
        if (face_lobe_is_held(head_held) && face_lobe_holder_slot(head_held) == slot &&
            (head_state & kLobeBig) != 0u) {
            face_lobe.words[face_lobe_header(head) + 1u] = frame;
            return;
        }
        broken = true;
        break;
    }
    if (broken) {
        for (uint way = 0u; way < kLobeWays; ++way) {
            const uint header = face_lobe_header(base + way);
            const uint held = face_lobe.words[header];
            if (!face_lobe_is_held(held) || face_lobe_holder_slot(held) != slot) continue;
            const uint state = face_lobe.words[face_lobe_state_at(base + way)];
            if ((state & (kLobeBig | kLobeFollower)) == 0u) continue;
            face_lobe.words[header] = 0u;
            face_lobe.words[face_lobe_state_at(base + way)] = 0u;
        }
    }

    // ---- four consecutive blocks of one set, taken whole -------------------------------------
    //
    // The same walk `node_face_lobe` makes for the same class, and it gives up quietly for the same
    // reason: a face that cannot get four keeps the one it has, which is a coarser lobe rather than
    // none. Taking four warm blocks off four faces to serve one is a trade nothing here can price.
    for (uint run = 0u; run + kLobeBigWays <= kLobeWays; run += kLobeBigWays) {
        bool free_run = true;
        for (uint way = run; way < run + kLobeBigWays; ++way) {
            const uint header = face_lobe_header(base + way);
            const uint held = face_lobe.words[header];
            if (!face_lobe_is_held(held)) continue;
            // My own cheap block inside the run is free to me: taking it back is not a take-over.
            if (face_lobe_holder_slot(held) == slot) continue;
            if (frame - face_lobe.words[header + 1u] <= kLobeCold) { free_run = false; break; }
        }
        if (!free_run) continue;

        const uint head = base + run;
        const uint header = face_lobe_header(head);
        const uint was = face_lobe.words[header];
        // One face may reach this for one slot, but two faces of one set reach it for two different
        // slots in the same dispatch, and the loser has to find out. The same compare-and-swap
        // `node_face_lobe` resolves that race with, and the loser simply keeps the block it had.
        if (atomicCompSwap(face_lobe.words[header], was, mine) != was) continue;

        // Whatever cheap block this face was holding OUTSIDE the run goes back now, and not before:
        // released above the swap it would be given up by a face that then failed to get a run, and
        // the next visit would hand it a fresh block and start its burst again for nothing.
        if (mine_at < kLobeWays && (mine_at < run || mine_at >= run + kLobeBigWays)) {
            face_lobe.words[face_lobe_header(base + mine_at)] = 0u;
            face_lobe.words[face_lobe_state_at(base + mine_at)] = 0u;
        }

        for (uint way = 1u; way < kLobeBigWays; ++way) {
            face_lobe.words[face_lobe_header(head + way)] = mine;
            face_lobe.words[face_lobe_header(head + way) + 1u] = frame;
            face_lobe.words[face_lobe_state_at(head + way)] = kLobeFollower;
        }
        face_lobe.words[header + 1u] = frame;
        face_lobe.words[face_lobe_state_at(head)] = kLobeBig;
        face_lobe.words[face_lobe_count_at(head)] = 0u;
        // Zeroed and not carried over: the run may have been four other faces' answers a moment
        // ago, and a weight of nought is exactly what tells every reader to fall back to the
        // hemispherical mean until this lobe has something of its own.
        for (uint bin = 0u; bin < kLobeBigBins; ++bin) {
            const uint at = face_lobe_bin_at(head, bin);
            if (at >= face_lobe.words.length()) return;
            face_lobe.words[at] = 0u;
            face_lobe.words[face_lobe_weight_at(head, bin, kLobeBigBins)] = 0u;
        }
        return;
    }
}

// One counter and one base per workgroup.
shared uint g_block_count;
shared uint g_block_base;
// Where each lane's slot goes within the block, so the write is one store with no second scan.
shared uint g_offsets[64];

void main() {
    const uint index = gl_LocalInvocationID.x;
    if (index == 0u) {
        g_block_count = 0u;
        g_block_base = 0u;
    }
    barrier();

    const uint slot = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    bool owed = false;
    if (slot < node_push.face_count) {
        // The same read the shading pass would have made, and the same decision. `false` for
        // `provisional_face` because this pass only ever covers the store's own range -- the card's
        // provisional tail is a separate, small, contiguous dispatch and is never compacted.
        const Face face = faces.items[slot];
        owed = face_work_of(slot, face, false).owed;
        // R4b: and how many outgoing bins this face has earned, which is asked of every LIVE face
        // and not only of the ones that owe work. A converged metal owes nothing and is still the
        // surface a reflection is read off, so a class decided only for the busy faces would give
        // the sharp class to a wall while it was settling and take it away the moment it settled.
        face_coverage_bins(slot, face);
    }

    // Where this lane sits in the block. An atomicAdd on a shared word is cheap and its ordering
    // between lanes does not matter.
    if (owed) g_offsets[index] = atomicAdd(g_block_count, 1u);
    barrier();

    if (index == 0u && g_block_count > 0u) {
        g_block_base = atomicAdd(face_work.count, g_block_count);
        // And the dispatch size, as a maximum over the blocks rather than a second pass.
        //
        // Every workgroup knows its own base and its own count, so the largest
        // ceil((base + count) / 64) any of them computes IS ceil(total / 64). One extra atomic per
        // workgroup, against a whole extra dispatch and a whole extra barrier to work out one
        // number the card already holds.
        atomicMax(face_work.groups_x, (g_block_base + g_block_count + 63u) / 64u);
    }
    barrier();

    if (!owed) return;
    const uint at = g_block_base + g_offsets[index];
    if (at >= face_work.slots.length()) return;   // the list is sized for the store; never hit
    face_work.slots[at] = slot;
}
